JavaScript is a high-level, interpreted programming language primarily used to make web pages interactive. Along with HTML (structure) and CSS (style), JavaScript is one of the core technologies of the web.

Originally created by Brendan Eich in 1995, JavaScript started as a simple scripting language for browsers. Today, it has evolved into a powerful, full-stack language used for frontend, backend, mobile apps, and even desktop applications.

🔹 Core Features of JavaScript
1. Dynamic and Weakly Typed

You don’t need to declare data types explicitly:

let x = 10;
x = "Hello"; // valid
2. Interpreted Language

JavaScript runs directly in the browser using engines like:

Google Chrome → V8 Engine
Mozilla Firefox → SpiderMonkey
3. Event-Driven Programming

JavaScript reacts to user actions:

clicks
typing
scrolling
4. Asynchronous Programming

JavaScript can run tasks without blocking:

Callbacks
Promises
Async/Await
5. Prototype-Based OOP

Instead of classical inheritance, JavaScript uses prototypes.

6. Runs Everywhere

Thanks to Node.js, JavaScript can run outside browsers (backend development).

🔹 JavaScript Uses
Web development (frontend)
Backend APIs (Node.js)
Mobile apps (React Native)
Desktop apps (Electron)
Game development
🔹 JavaScript + DOM

The most important use of JavaScript in browsers is interacting with the DOM (Document Object Model).

🌳 What is DOM?

The DOM (Document Object Model) is a programming interface for HTML and XML documents. It represents the page as a tree structure, where each element is a node.

Example:

<body>
  <h1>Hello</h1>
</body>

This becomes:

Document
 └── Body
      └── h1

JavaScript uses the DOM to:

Access elements
Modify content
Change styles
Handle events
📘 25 DOM Questions and Answers
1. What is DOM?

The DOM is a structured representation of an HTML document as a tree of objects. It allows JavaScript to access and manipulate elements dynamically, making web pages interactive.

2. What is document object?

The document object represents the entire HTML page. It is the root of the DOM and provides methods to access elements, modify content, and handle events.

3. What is getElementById()?

This method selects an element using its unique ID. It returns a single element object which can then be modified or styled using JavaScript.

4. What is getElementsByClassName()?

It selects multiple elements with the same class name and returns an HTMLCollection. You can loop through it to apply changes to each element.

5. What is querySelector()?

This method selects the first element that matches a CSS selector. It is flexible and supports complex selectors like classes, IDs, and tags.

6. What is querySelectorAll()?

It returns all elements matching a CSS selector as a NodeList. You can iterate through it using loops like forEach().

7. Difference between HTMLCollection and NodeList?

HTMLCollection is live (updates automatically), while NodeList is static in most cases. NodeList also supports forEach, while HTMLCollection does not directly.

8. What is innerHTML?

It allows you to get or set HTML content inside an element. It can insert full HTML but may lead to security issues if not handled carefully.

9. What is textContent?

It returns only text inside an element without HTML tags. It is safer and faster compared to innerHTML.

10. What is style property?

It allows you to apply inline CSS styles directly to an element using JavaScript.

11. What is classList?

It is used to add, remove, or toggle CSS classes on an element, making it easier to manage styling dynamically.

12. What is createElement()?

This method creates a new HTML element dynamically, which can then be added to the DOM.

13. What is appendChild()?

It adds a new child element to a parent element at the end of its children list.

14. What is removeChild()?

It removes a child element from a parent node.

15. What is replaceChild()?

It replaces one child element with another within a parent node.

16. What is Event in DOM?

An event is an action like clicking, typing, or hovering. JavaScript listens and responds to these events.

17. What is addEventListener()?

It attaches an event handler to an element without overwriting existing handlers, making event handling more flexible.

18. What is Event Bubbling?

It is when an event starts from the target element and propagates upward through its parent elements.

19. What is Event Capturing?

It is the opposite of bubbling; the event starts from the root and travels down to the target element.

20. What is preventDefault()?

It stops the default action of an element, like preventing form submission or link navigation.

21. What is stopPropagation()?

It prevents the event from propagating (bubbling or capturing) further in the DOM tree.

22. What is setAttribute()?

It sets or updates an attribute on an HTML element, such as class, id, or src.

23. What is getAttribute()?

It retrieves the value of an attribute from an element.

24. What is parentNode?

It returns the parent element of a given node, allowing traversal upward in the DOM tree.

25. What is childNodes?

It returns all child nodes of an element, including text nodes, comments, and elements.
